## What's happening?
The OPW source `{{component_id.name}}` in pipeline `{{pipeline_name.name}}` spent an average of **{{value}} seconds** blocked waiting for the pipeline to accept a batch over the last 10 minutes, above the 5 second threshold. This is the most direct measure of backpressure: the source cannot hand work off, so inbound requests queue behind it.
{{/is_alert}}{{#is_warning}}
## What's happening?
The OPW source `{{component_id.name}}` in pipeline `{{pipeline_name.name}}` spent an average of **{{value}} seconds** blocked waiting for the pipeline to accept a batch over the last 10 minutes, above the 1 second warning threshold. Backpressure is building but the source is still keeping up.
{{/is_warning}}
{{#is_recovery}}Source send latency recovered for `{{component_id.name}}` in pipeline `{{pipeline_name.name}}`.{{/is_recovery}}

## Reading this correctly
`pipelines.source_send_batch_latency_seconds` is a **distribution**. This monitor uses `avg:`, so it reports the mean blocked time and describes the pipeline's condition. It does not tell you whether data is being lost: individual batches can cross the client's exporter deadline and be cancelled while the mean stays well below this threshold. The companion monitor on the tail covers that case.

Expect this monitor to be in continuous alarm, not flapping, if the pipeline has entered a sustained backpressure plateau. Blocked time stabilises once the source's residence time stretches far enough that throughput matches the drain rate, so a flat multi-second value is the normal shape of this failure rather than a sign of measurement error.

